  "question": {
    "text": "At some location&nbsp; the horizontal component of earth&#39;s magnetic field is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>18</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mo>.</mo></math> At this location, magnetic needle of length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0.12</mn><mi>&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> and pole strength <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1.8</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Am</mi></math> is suspended from its mid-point using a thread, it makes <math><mn>45</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math> angles with horizontal in equilibrium. To keep this needle horizontal, the vertical force that should be applied at one of its ends is:"
  },
